---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/fashion';
const title = "Choosing Shopify email apps for fashion stores";
const desc = "Choose an email app by how it handles collection launches, exact size availability, exchanges, and the transition from full-price selling to clearance. The merchandising calendar and the returns queue need to be part of the same decision.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does every apparel store need a dedicated platform?",
    "answer": "Compare the actual drop, size, and exchange requirements with your current setup. A demonstrated gap is more useful than an order-count cutoff."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should an order overwrite a saved size?",
    "answer": "A gift or exchange can make that inference wrong. Keep a stated preference and purchase history distinguishable."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should winback start?",
    "answer": "Work back from the real merchandising calendar and available sizes, and stop or revise the sequence when the offer changes."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/for">All store types</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Define the launch inventory contract</h2>
      <p>For each drop, name the collection, variant identifiers, eligible audience, opening time and time zone, and person who can stop a send. Distinguish an early-access invitation from reserved stock. A customer receiving a link should not be told an item is held unless the store actually holds it.</p>
      <p>Merchandising owns availability and launch changes; marketing owns the audience and message. Document who updates both when a shipment is late or a size run is incomplete. Keep an evergreen welcome path available when a launch is postponed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Capture collection interest and revise it</h2>
      <p>A waitlist form should record the collection requested and what alert the shopper expects. Offer a separate choice for ongoing marketing. Someone waiting for one capsule should not be treated as interested in every future drop.</p>
      <p>Size preferences are optional and can change. A gift order or a purchase in another size should not silently overwrite a stated preference. Store the source and date so the operator can resolve a conflicting record rather than guessing which size is correct.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Check the exact variant before recovery</h2>
      <p>Cart and back-in-stock messages need the variant the shopper requested, not merely its parent product. If size M is unavailable, do not render size L as though it is the same recovery opportunity. Provide a clear alternative or leave out the product block.</p>
      <p>A send-time stock check and the destination page can still disagree when inventory moves quickly. Ask the supplier to show the fallback and cancellation behavior. A back-in-stock email is an availability notification, not a stock reservation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Coordinate VIP and public access</h2>
      <p>Define VIP eligibility from the store’s actual loyalty or purchase policy and state the benefit accurately. Keep the public launch out of the early-access audience until the correct time. Recheck for purchase before the next promotional step.</p>
      <p>Give email and SMS one contact plan even if different systems send them. A subscriber who already bought the drop should not receive an escalating sequence asking them to buy it again. Set frequency rules for your calendar and audience rather than copying a universal cap.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Put fit support before a second sale</h2>
      <p>Link the purchased style to its measurements, care instructions, and actual exchange policy. A measurement guide for denim is different from one for a structured jacket. Delivery status matters more than a fixed delay after checkout when deciding whether the customer has had time to try the item.</p>
      <p>Route an open exchange or return into support and pause conflicting cross-sell. A replacement order should not restart a new-customer sequence. Keep return reason information factual and limited to what is needed for the next useful message.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Keep exchange inventory separate from promotion</h2>
      <p>A returned small and replacement medium can appear as two orders in a shallow integration. Ask how the app identifies the original line, replacement line, refund, and current fulfillment state. Deduplicate the lifecycle enrollment at the customer and order relationship level.</p>
      <p>Support should decide when a fit issue is resolved. Marketing can then offer a revised fit guide or a different collection if the customer wants it. Avoid labeling all customers who return an item as discount shoppers or excluding them permanently from useful content.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Plan winback against the actual markdown calendar</h2>
      <p>Before clearance, choose an edit relevant to the prior collection or stated style preference. Verify that the featured sizes remain available. Stop the sequence when prices change so an earlier full-price message does not conflict with the live offer.</p>
      <p>Show merchandising and finance which products will carry an incentive and how code stacking works. A proposed comparison of styled content and an offer can inform future decisions, but neither path comes with a promised conversion or return reduction.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Require a fashion-specific supplier demonstration</h2>
      <p>Give Sequenzy, Klaviyo, Omnisend, Drip, or another candidate the same drop brief. Ask each to show a size-specific waitlist, purchase suppression, public launch exclusion, and exchange handling. If Privy or another capture tool stays, include its actual data handoff.</p>
      <p>Keep Shopify as the native comparison point before adding another subscription. Request written pricing at expected contact growth and peak launch volume, including SMS and connector costs. Record missing fields, manual steps, and who can maintain the setup; do not select by an unsupported ranking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Rehearse a changed drop before moving senders</h2>
      <p>Use staff records for a VIP who buys early, a shopper whose size sells out, an exchange customer, and someone who withdraws permission while queued. Change the launch time after scheduling and inspect every affected message and destination. These are proposed checks, not reported merchant outcomes.</p>
      <p>Migrate one collection path at a time. Preview the new trigger without live duplicate sends, then pause the old equivalent before enabling it. Save the incumbent configuration and a rollback owner so a broken inventory mapping does not require improvisation on launch day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Read results after returns settle</h2>
      <p>Compare contribution after discounts, refunds, return handling, and platform costs by collection and entry source. Separate full-price and markdown orders and use a maturity window appropriate to your actual return policy. Opens alone do not show whether fit support helped.</p>
      <p>Review size-block failures, exchange collisions, unsubscribes, and operator effort alongside revenue. A larger list or catalog is a planning input, not an automatic migration threshold. Switch when a demonstrated requirement and its maintainable implementation justify the total cost.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Keep the native baseline in the comparison</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify documents Shopify Messaging, formerly Shopify Email</a>, for campaigns and marketing automations. Compare your actual requirements with the setup available to your store before deciding to replace it. The baseline does not establish that a particular edge case works; demonstrate that case with your own field mapping.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Build a selection brief</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">plan the migration</a>, and <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">review incentive costs</a>. Keep the failed requirement, responsible owner, and proposed next step in the decision record.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Store email questions" />
</BaseLayout>
